[2026-06-01 10:27:53] [SUBPROCESS] top_suggestion: Chapter 14 ('Os Maus Fecham-SE') tries to do too much in too little space — corporate pressure, Renan's public betrayal, Elisa's severe insomnia, AND the anonymous letter all arrive within 2,917 words. The anonymous letter in particular ('A sua mãe não terminou a equação') is the most personally threatening moment in the entire novel and deserves more room to breathe. Consider splitting this material across two chapters: one focused on the external pressures (corporations, Renan, media) and another focused on the internal collapse (insomnia, the letter, Elisa's confrontation with the guardanapos). This would give the 'All Is Lost' sequence more structural weight and prevent the middle of Act II from feeling rushed compared to the more measured pacing of the surrounding chapters.
